The `sqlTag` function will be implemented as a tagged template literal processor in JavaScript. It will handle various types of interpolations: simple values, nested `sqlTag` results, and arrays. For nested `sqlTag` results, their SQL text will be inlined into the parent query, and their placeholders will be renumbered to avoid conflicts. Arrays will be expanded into comma-separated lists of placeholders. Each unique value will receive a separate placeholder, even if it appears multiple times. This ensures that the resulting SQL query is correctly parameterized and avoids SQL injection risks.

ANSWER:

```javascript
module.exports = function sqlTag(strings, ...values) {
  let text = strings[0];
  const valuesArray = [];
  let placeholderIndex = 1;

  values.forEach((value, i) => {
    if (typeof value === 'object' && value !== null && value.text) {
      // Inline nested sqlTag result
      const nestedText = value.text;
      const nestedValues = value.values;
      nestedText.split('$').forEach((part, index) => {
        if (index > 0) {
          const num = parseInt(part, 10);
          text += `$${placeholderIndex + num - 1}`;
          valuesArray.push(nestedValues[num - 1]);
        } else {
          text += part;
        }
      });
      placeholderIndex += nestedValues.length;
    } else if (Array.isArray(value)) {
      // Handle array by creating a comma-separated list of placeholders
      const placeholders = value.map(() => `$${placeholderIndex++}`);
      text += placeholders.join(', ');
      valuesArray.push(...value);
    } else {
      // Handle simple value
      text += `$${placeholderIndex++}`;
      valuesArray.push(value);
    }
  });

  return { text, values: valuesArray };
};
```

